\begin_layout Title
Does Marijuana Consumption Impair College Performance?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 3, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Marijuana is the most commonly used illicit drug in the US.
 The legalization of marijuana has become a topic of fierce debate in recent
 months due to Colorado and Washington amending their constitutions to allow
 its recreational use.
 In these states, consumption of cannabis is permitted in a manner similar
 to alcohol, and is legal to sell cannabis provided the store has obtained
 a permit to do so.
 Advocates of the measure claim that legalizing marijuana would end the
 black market, generate huge tax revenues, and reduce law enforcement costs.
 However, those who oppose legalization vehmently claim that the costs to
 society marijuana would enduce are much greater than the revenue it will
 generate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With my project, I intend to measure the effect marijuana consumption has
 on collegiate performance.
 I wish to focus on the population of college students because surveys consisten
tly show that marijuana use and abuse among college students is higher than
 estimates from the general population.
 The notable problem facing this examination is the fact that the treatment
 variable is endogenous.
 We cannot rule out the fact that consuming marijuana may be correlated
 with some omitted variable, which also has an affect on grades.
 To solve this problem, I will employ an instrumental variable to isolate
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

as-good-as-random
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation in the treatment variable.
 The instrumental variable I propose is the price of marijuana around the
 college campuses of interest.
 This instrumental variable is correlated with the endogenous variable because
 the price of marijuana affects consumption.
 Yet, I cannot formulate a relevant story in which the price of marijuana
 affects has an effect on the grades of students.
 So, the only way this instrument can have an effect on the outcome is through
 the treatment variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The legalizaiton of marijuana in Colorado and Washington will take effect
 on January 1, 2014.
 Consequently, I am unable to conduct my analysis until enough time has
 been given for the law to influence behavior, so data can become readily
 available.
 This includes commericial stores electing to sell marijuana in the region
 of college campuses.
 I will also need to focus on colleges that also change their drug policies
 in accordance with the new laws.
 In order to obtain the necessary data, I will conduct a survey of randomly
 selected college students.
 This will be a panel data setup in which I control for individual and school
 fixed effects.
 Important to note, the primary treatment of interest must focus on consumption
 of marijuana while in college.
 While it is important to determine if a student consistently consumers
 marijuana, the research does not attempt to answer if the exact frequency
 of marijuana consumption affects school performance.
\end_layout

